// CRES Academy question bank, units 1 to 4. All questions are original.
// Fields: id, t (lesson id), q (stem), a (four options), c (index of correct option), e (explanation).
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u1l1
{id:"u1l1-1",t:"u1l1",q:"A service manual for a radiographic table tells you to level the detector to the midcoronal plane. Which division of the body does that plane describe?",a:["Vertically into equal left and right halves","Vertically into front and back parts","Horizontally into upper and lower parts","Along the long axis of a limb"],c:1,e:"Coronal planes divide the body vertically into anterior and posterior parts, and the midcoronal plane runs through the middle of that split. Sagittal divides left from right and transverse divides upper from lower."},
{id:"u1l1-2",t:"u1l1",q:"A radiographer asks you to check the tube lock because the left lateral decubitus images look tilted. What does that position require of the equipment?",a:["A vertical central ray with the patient erect","A central ray angled along the long axis of the part","A horizontal central ray with the patient lying down","A rotated patient with the tube angled to match"],c:2,e:"Decubitus means the patient is lying down and the central ray is horizontal, which is what makes air and fluid levels visible. It needs a tube that locks true horizontal, a vertical detector, and locks that hold."},
{id:"u1l1-3",t:"u1l1",q:"Technique charts in a general room are built around one body build. Which habitus do they assume, and what share of patients does it cover?",a:["Hypersthenic, about 5 percent","Asthenic, about 10 percent","Hyposthenic, about 35 percent","Sthenic, about 50 percent"],c:3,e:"Sthenic is the average athletic build and about 50 percent of patients, so charts are written for it. Hypersthenic is about 5 percent, hyposthenic about 35, and asthenic about 10."},
{id:"u1l1-4",t:"u1l1",q:"Radiology reports that gallbladder studies on large patients are often centered off in one room. What is the MOST likely explanation?",a:["Organ position shifts with body habitus, so centering changes with build","The light field no longer matches the radiation field","The table detents have drifted out of adjustment","The detector is mounted at the wrong height"],c:0,e:"Between hypersthenic and asthenic builds, organs such as the gallbladder and stomach shift 6 to 8 inches, which is more than the collimated field. Prove the lamp, light field, and distance indicator, then the remainder is a technique and centering judgment."},
{id:"u1l1-5",t:"u1l1",q:"Reading a positioning procedure, you see that the wrist should be placed distal to the point being measured. What does distal mean?",a:["Toward the midline of the body","Farther from the trunk or point of attachment","Toward the head","Toward the front of the body"],c:1,e:"Proximal means nearer the trunk and distal means farther from it, and both are relative: the knee is distal to the hip and proximal to the ankle at the same time."},

// u1l2
{id:"u1l2-1",t:"u1l2",q:"A radiologist reports that hearts look larger on images from one chest room than from the others. Which equipment factor should you check FIRST?",a:["The grid ratio installed in the chest stand","The detector gain calibration","The source-to-image distance and the projection used","The automatic exposure control cell selection"],c:2,e:"Magnification is the source-to-image distance divided by the source-to-object distance, about 1.1 at 100 cm and 1.05 at 180 cm, and an anteroposterior projection puts the heart farther from the detector. Distance and projection change apparent heart size before any image-processing factor does."},
{id:"u1l2-2",t:"u1l2",q:"Chest radiographs are routinely made during full inspiration. Why does that breathing instruction matter to the image?",a:["It holds the heart still during the exposure","It reduces the amount of scatter the grid must remove","It lets the automatic exposure control settle","It lowers the diaphragm and shows the most aerated lung"],c:3,e:"On full inspiration the lungs are largest and the diaphragm lowest, so the most lung is visible. An expiration image looks crowded and is usually repeated, and that is a breathing instruction rather than a machine setting."},
{id:"u1l2-3",t:"u1l2",q:"An order calls for a contrast study of the lower gastrointestinal tract by enema. Which agent belongs in that study?",a:["Barium sulfate","Iodinated contrast given by vein","A gadolinium-based agent","Sterile saline"],c:0,e:"Barium sulfate is dense, is not absorbed, and stays in the tract, so it is swallowed or given as an enema for gastrointestinal studies. Iodinated contrast goes into vessels and the urinary system instead."},
{id:"u1l2-4",t:"u1l2",q:"While tracing a urinary contrast study complaint you need to name the path urine takes. Which sequence is correct?",a:["Kidney, bladder, ureter, urethra","Kidney, ureter, bladder, urethra","Ureter, kidney, urethra, bladder","Kidney, urethra, bladder, ureter"],c:1,e:"The kidneys filter blood, the ureters carry urine down to the bladder, the bladder stores it, and the urethra passes it out. Knowing the order is how you follow which part of a contrast study is being imaged."},
{id:"u1l2-5",t:"u1l2",q:"Comparing the technique requirements of a chest exam with those of an abdominal exam, which statement is correct?",a:["Both use the same kilovoltage, and only the time differs","Chest work needs a grid while abdominal work does not","Chest work uses a high kilovoltage above 100 kVp while abdominal work relies on a grid for the scatter a thick trunk produces","Abdominal work uses the longer source-to-image distance"],c:2,e:"Chest imaging needs latitude through lung and mediastinum, so it uses a high kilovoltage technique above 100 kVp at the long distance. An abdomen is thick and scatters heavily, so it needs a grid and the shortest practical exposure time because bowel moves."},

// u1l3
{id:"u1l3-1",t:"u1l3",q:"A technique chart groups exams by region. Which bones make up the axial skeleton?",a:["The limbs and the girdles that attach them","The flat and sesamoid bones wherever they occur","Only the vertebral column and the pelvis","Skull, vertebral column, ribs, and sternum"],c:3,e:"The axial skeleton is the central column of the body: skull, vertebral column, ribs, and sternum. The appendicular skeleton is the limbs plus the shoulder and pelvic girdles."},
{id:"u1l3-2",t:"u1l3",q:"An automatic exposure control complaint arises during a full spine series. How many vertebrae are in each region of the column?",a:["7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 fused sacral, and a coccyx","5 cervical, 7 thoracic, 12 lumbar, 5 fused sacral, and a coccyx","12 cervical, 7 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 4 fused sacral, and a coccyx","7 cervical, 5 thoracic, 12 lumbar, 5 fused sacral, and a coccyx"],c:0,e:"The column runs 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 fused sacral, and a coccyx of 4 fused bones, 33 in all. A spine series therefore crosses more thickness change than any other exam, which is what tests the exposure control."},
{id:"u1l3-3",t:"u1l3",q:"Classifying the patella for a positioning discussion, which of the five primary bone types does it belong to?",a:["A long bone","A sesamoid bone","A flat bone","An irregular bone"],c:1,e:"The five primary bone types are long such as the femur, short such as the carpals, flat such as the ribs, sesamoid such as the patella, and irregular such as the vertebrae."},
{id:"u1l3-4",t:"u1l3",q:"Most joint imaging is ordered for the freely movable joints. How are those classified by structure?",a:["Fibrous","Cartilaginous","Synovial","Sesamoid"],c:2,e:"By structure joints are fibrous (immovable, such as skull sutures), cartilaginous (slightly movable, such as the discs between vertebral bodies), or synovial, which have a capsule and fluid and are the freely movable ones."},
{id:"u1l3-5",t:"u1l3",q:"A radiologist complains that extremity images from one room lack fine detail at the cortex. Which factors should you examine?",a:["The detector offset calibration only","Grid ratio and grid frequency","Kilovoltage and automatic exposure control density steps","Focal spot size, object-to-image distance, and motion"],c:3,e:"Thin, low-scatter parts are judged on recorded detail, which is set by focal spot size, how close the part is to the detector, and whether anything moved. Grids and higher kilovoltage belong to thick, scattering anatomy instead."},

// u1l4
{id:"u1l4-1",t:"u1l4",q:"A procedure calls for a posteroanterior projection. Where does the central ray enter and leave the patient?",a:["It enters the back and exits the front","It enters the front and exits the back","It crosses from one side to the other","It travels along the long axis of the part"],c:0,e:"Posteroanterior means posterior to anterior: the beam enters the back and leaves the front, with the patient facing the receptor. That places the anatomy closer to the detector and magnifies it less, which is why the standard erect chest image is made this way."},
{id:"u1l4-2",t:"u1l4",q:"During a fluoroscopic gastrointestinal study the radiographer asks for a right anterior oblique. Which part of the patient faces the receptor?",a:["The right side of the back","The right front of the body","The left side of the back","The left front of the body"],c:1,e:"In an oblique name the first letter gives the side and the second whether the front or back of that side is against the receptor, so a right anterior oblique puts the right front to the detector. The tube and receptor stay square while the patient rotates."},
{id:"u1l4-3",t:"u1l4",q:"Checking a room's distance indicator, you need the standard source-to-image distances. Which pair is correct?",a:["About 50 cm for routine work and 100 cm for chest","About 180 cm for routine work and 100 cm for chest","About 100 cm for routine work and 180 cm for chest","About 90 cm for all exams"],c:2,e:"Routine radiography uses about 100 cm and chest work 180 cm, with about 90 cm on some special units. The long chest distance limits magnification of the heart so that its measured width stays meaningful."},
{id:"u1l4-4",t:"u1l4",q:"A skull procedure specifies a tube angle measured from the line between the outer corner of the eye and the ear canal. Which line is that?",a:["The glabellomeatal line","The infraorbitomeatal line","The acanthiomeatal line","The orbitomeatal line"],c:3,e:"The orbitomeatal line runs from the outer canthus of the eye to the auricular point at the ear canal. The glabellomeatal line starts between the eyebrows and the acanthiomeatal line at the junction of nose and upper lip."},
{id:"u1l4-5",t:"u1l4",q:"After a console software update, images leave the room without the date of birth in the overlay. How should you treat this?",a:["As a compliance problem, because identifying markers on the image are required","As a cosmetic preference the department can decide about","As a problem only if the archive rejects the images","As acceptable while the name and identifier are still present"],c:0,e:"Every image must carry the patient name, a unique identifier, date of birth, the date made, anatomic markers, orientation, any time interval, inspiration or expiration for a chest, and sequence markers for a series. A missing field is a compliance problem, not a preference."},

// u2l1
{id:"u2l1-1",t:"u2l1",q:"A technologist reports feeling a tingle from a cassette holder on a table. Which current level does a just-perceptible tingle correspond to, and what should you do?",a:["About 10 mA, and the device may stay in use until the next PM","About 1 mA, and the device comes out of service for testing","About 100 mA, and the room is evacuated","About 5 A, and the breaker should be replaced"],c:1,e:"The threshold of perception is about 1 mA. A tingle is proof of a real leakage path, so the device leaves service for ground and leakage testing; 5 mA is the accepted harmless maximum, 10 to 20 mA is let-go, and 100 to 300 mA is the fibrillation range."},
{id:"u2l1-2",t:"u2l1",q:"Testing a mobile imaging unit with a safety analyzer, you measure 1.4 ohm from the chassis to the plug ground pin. How should you read that result?",a:["A pass, because the limit applies only to fixed equipment","A pass, because the limit is 5 ohm","A fail, because NFPA 99 allows no more than 0.5 ohm on patient-care equipment","A fail, because the limit is 0.05 ohm"],c:2,e:"NFPA 99 sets ground resistance from chassis to plug ground pin at 0.5 ohm maximum for patient-care equipment. A reading nearly three times that points at a worn plug, a damaged cord, or a loose internal ground strap."},
{id:"u2l1-3",t:"u2l1",q:"An interventional suite uses applied parts connected directly to the heart. Which IEC 60601-1 patient leakage limits apply to them?",a:["100 microamps normal and 500 single fault","5 mA normal and 10 mA single fault","500 microamps normal and 1 mA single fault","10 microamps normal and 50 microamps single fault"],c:3,e:"Type CF, cardiac floating, carries the tightest limits in IEC 60601-1 at 10 microamps normal and 50 microamps single fault, because as little as 10 microamps delivered directly to the heart can cause fibrillation. Type B and BF parts are allowed 100 and 500 microamps."},
{id:"u2l1-4",t:"u2l1",q:"You are about to open the high-voltage cabinet of a generator you have already disconnected and locked out. What must happen before a hand goes inside?",a:["Wait the manual's interval, discharge the capacitors with the manufacturer's tool, and verify zero with a meter","Nothing further, because the disconnect is locked open","Energize the system briefly to bleed the capacitors through the tube","Remove the interlock so the cabinet can be opened safely"],c:0,e:"Capacitors hold charge after the disconnect is opened, and a capacitor-discharge unit stores a whole exposure's worth. Wait the stated bleed-down interval, discharge each capacitor to ground with the proper tool, then prove zero with a meter before touching anything."},
{id:"u2l1-5",t:"u2l1",q:"During a PM you find that a C-arm collision sensor has been taped over so a case could be finished. What is the correct action?",a:["Note it for the next visit, since the operators clearly manage without it","Restore the sensor, prove it works, remove the unit from service if it cannot be restored, and document the finding","Leave it, because collision sensors slow clinical work","Disable the matching sensor on the other side so motion is symmetrical"],c:1,e:"A defeated collision sensor is a patient and equipment safety defect, not a comfort item. Restore it, prove it stops the motion, document the defeat so it can be addressed as a practice issue, and keep the unit out of service until it is right."},

// u2l2
{id:"u2l2-1",t:"u2l2",q:"A visiting physicist reports a cumulative reading of 2 rem for a staff member. What is that in millisieverts?",a:["2 mSv","200 mSv","20 mSv","0.2 mSv"],c:2,e:"1 Sv equals 100 rem, so 1 rem is 10 mSv and 2 rem is 20 mSv. The same factor of 100 links the gray and the rad: 1 Gy equals 100 rad."},
{id:"u2l2-2",t:"u2l2",q:"A 30-year-old technologist asks what their lifetime occupational limit is. Which value applies and how is it found?",a:["50 mSv, the annual limit","150 mSv, from the lens limit","500 mSv, from the extremity limit","300 mSv, from 10 mSv times age in years"],c:3,e:"The cumulative occupational limit is 10 mSv times age in years, so 10 x 30 = 300 mSv, alongside the annual effective dose limit of 50 mSv. The lens limit is 150 mSv a year and skin and extremities 500 mSv a year."},
{id:"u2l2-3",t:"u2l2",q:"A radiographer in a fluoroscopy suite declares her pregnancy. Which limit now applies to the embryo or fetus?",a:["0.5 mSv in any month","5 mSv in any month","1 mSv per year","50 mSv over the gestation"],c:0,e:"Once a pregnancy is declared the limit is 0.5 mSv in any month to the embryo or fetus, with 5 mSv over the whole gestation. A second badge is worn at the waist under the apron to monitor it."},
{id:"u2l2-4",t:"u2l2",q:"Where is a personnel dosimetry badge worn during fluoroscopic work, and why there?",a:["At the waist under the apron, to record the trunk dose","At the collar outside the apron, because that reads what reaches the unprotected neck, eyes, and thyroid","On the wrist, to record extremity dose","Clipped inside a pocket, to protect it from damage"],c:1,e:"The badge goes at the collar outside the apron, because the limits that bite in fluoroscopy are for the lens of the eye and the thyroid, which the apron does not cover. A declared pregnant worker adds a second badge at the waist under the apron."},
{id:"u2l2-5",t:"u2l2",q:"Explaining a dose report to a nervous staff member, what is the approximate annual natural background dose?",a:["About 0.3 mSv","About 30 mSv","About 3 mSv","About 300 mSv"],c:2,e:"Natural background is about 3 mSv a year, radon being the largest part, and the largest artificial contribution to population dose is medical imaging. One chest image is about 0.02 mSv, which is the comparison that makes a badge report understandable."},

// u2l3
{id:"u2l3-1",t:"u2l3",q:"A scatter survey reads 16 mGy/h at 1 m from the patient during fluoroscopy. What is the rate at 2 m?",a:["8 mGy/h","32 mGy/h","2 mGy/h","4 mGy/h"],c:3,e:"Intensity falls with the square of the distance: 16 x (1 / 2) squared = 16 / 4 = 4 mGy/h. Doubling the distance leaves one quarter of the rate, which is why one step back beats any apron."},
{id:"u2l3-2",t:"u2l3",q:"Standing at 3 m from a source, a staff member reads 2 microGy/h. Approximately what would they read at 1 m?",a:["18 microGy/h","9 microGy/h","6 microGy/h","0.7 microGy/h"],c:0,e:"Moving closer multiplies by the square of the distance ratio: 2 x (3 / 1) squared = 2 x 9 = 18 microGy/h. Write down which way the distance is going before squaring, because the law works both ways."},
{id:"u2l3-3",t:"u2l3",q:"Partway through a long fluoroscopic case an audible signal sounds. What has the signal reported?",a:["The tabletop exposure rate has exceeded 10 R/min","Five minutes of cumulative beam-on time","The tube has reached its thermal limit","The automatic brightness control has reached its limit"],c:1,e:"The fluoroscopic cumulative timer gives an audible signal at 5 minutes of beam-on time, and beam-on time is recorded for the exam. It is a prompt to reconsider the case, not a fault."},
{id:"u2l3-4",t:"u2l3",q:"A fluoroscopy room's measured dose rates have crept up since its last survey. Which operator-side factors raise dose rate the MOST?",a:["Collimating tightly to the anatomy","Using last-image hold instead of a second look","Continuous rather than pulsed mode, and a magnification mode left engaged","Keeping the receptor close to the patient"],c:2,e:"Pulsed mode is the largest saving an operator controls, and magnification uses less of the receptor so the dose rate rises to maintain brightness. Last-image hold, collimation, and a close receptor all lower dose."},
{id:"u2l3-5",t:"u2l3",q:"An inspector asks the department to demonstrate ALARA. Which of these is the physical-action evidence a technician supplies?",a:["The written policy statement adopted by the committee","Pediatric technique charts and protocols by patient size","Minutes of the radiation safety committee meeting","Working collimator lamps, accurate light fields, inspected apparel, and proved interlocks with the readings recorded"],c:3,e:"ALARA shows up as policy, procedure, and physical action. Policy belongs to administration and protocols to radiology and the physicist; measured, recorded equipment performance is the technician's layer, and it is the one a surveyor can verify fastest."},

// u2l4
{id:"u2l4-1",t:"u2l4",q:"Specifying replacement lead aprons for an interventional team, what lead equivalent is the minimum and what is the normal choice?",a:["0.25 mm minimum, 0.5 mm normal","0.1 mm minimum, 0.25 mm normal","0.5 mm minimum, 1 mm normal","1 mm minimum, 2 mm normal"],c:0,e:"Aprons and gloves come in 0.25, 0.5, and 1 mm lead equivalent, with 0.25 mm the minimum and 0.5 mm the usual choice. A 1 mm apron attenuates about 99 percent at 75 kVp but can weigh 12 kg, which is why 0.5 mm is the working compromise."},
{id:"u2l4-2",t:"u2l4",q:"How often must protective aprons, gloves, and shields be checked, and by what method?",a:["Monthly, by visual inspection alone","At least annually, by imaging them with fluoroscopy or radiography","Every three years, by weighing them","Only when a user reports a problem"],c:1,e:"Every apron, glove, thyroid shield, and gonadal shield is imaged at least annually, by fluoroscopy or by radiography at about 120 kVp and 10 mAs, and retired for cracks, tears, or holes in critical areas. A crack from folding is invisible from the outside, which is why a visual check alone is not enough."},
{id:"u2l4-3",t:"u2l4",q:"Comparing a fluoroscopic receptor assembly with a control booth wall, which statement about barriers is correct?",a:["Both are primary barriers and need the same lead equivalent","The booth is the primary barrier because the operator stands behind it","The receptor assembly is a primary barrier of at least 2 mm while the booth is a secondary barrier of about 1/16 inch","Neither is a barrier; they are convenience shielding"],c:2,e:"A primary barrier intercepts the useful beam, so the fluoroscopic receptor assembly is one at at least 2 mm lead equivalent and is interlocked against energizing while parked. A secondary barrier such as the booth only sees scatter and leakage, so it can be much thinner."},
{id:"u2l4-4",t:"u2l4",q:"A medical physicist is calculating the shielding for a new radiographic room. Which four inputs drive that design?",a:["Patient throughput, staffing, budget, and schedule","Kilovoltage, milliamperage, time, and grid ratio","Room size, ceiling height, door width, and flooring","Workload, use factor, occupancy factor, and distance"],c:3,e:"Shielding reports are computed from the room's workload, the use factor for how much beam-on time points at that barrier, the occupancy factor for who is behind it and for how long, and the distance. Any wall penetration you make later is a change to that design and must be sealed and surveyed."},
{id:"u2l4-5",t:"u2l4",q:"A collimator lamp has been out in a busy room for several weeks. How should that be prioritized?",a:["As a dose-critical fault, because collimation is the first shield and the radiographer centers to the lamp","As cosmetic, since the beam still collimates to the set field","As a scheduling item for the next annual survey","As a detector calibration issue"],c:0,e:"Tissue outside the field receives only scatter, so collimation protects more than any garment, and the radiographer sets the field using the lamp. A dark lamp invites an oversized field on every exam, which makes it an urgent dose issue."},

// u2l5
{id:"u2l5-1",t:"u2l5",q:"Before entering the controlled areas of a magnetic resonance suite, where must ferromagnetic screening be complete?",a:["At the door of the magnet room","Before anyone crosses the posted 5-gauss line","Only for patients, not for staff or service personnel","After the scanner has been placed in standby"],c:1,e:"Screening is complete before the 5-gauss line, because that is where implants and pacemakers can be disturbed. The field is never off, 1 T equals 10,000 gauss, and a common clinical magnet is 3 T, so the fringe field reaches further than people expect."},
{id:"u2l5-2",t:"u2l5",q:"You need to take an electrical safety analyzer into a magnetic resonance scan room for a rated accessory. What governs whether that is allowed?",a:["Whether the analyzer is carried rather than wheeled","Whether the scanner is between patients","The analyzer's labeling as MR Safe, MR Conditional, or MR Unsafe, with conditional items used only within their stated limits","Whether a technologist accompanies you"],c:2,e:"Everything taken near the magnet carries one of three labels, and most meters, analyzers, carts, and hand tools are MR Unsafe. Conditional items may be used only within the field, gradient, and radiofrequency limits they were tested to, and the magnet staff are involved every time."},
{id:"u2l5-3",t:"u2l5",q:"A technologist asks when the quench button should be pressed. What is the correct answer?",a:["Whenever service work inside the bore would be easier without the field","Whenever the helium level alarm sounds","At the end of every working day","Only to free someone pinned by a projectile or in another life-threatening emergency"],c:3,e:"A quench boils off the helium, vents enormous volumes of cold gas, can displace oxygen if it releases into the room, costs a great deal, and can damage the magnet. It is an emergency measure, and a helium alarm is a service call instead."},
{id:"u2l5-4",t:"u2l5",q:"A patient reports a skin burn along a monitoring cable after a magnetic resonance scan. What is the MOST likely mechanism?",a:["Deposited radiofrequency energy concentrating in a looped or skin-contacting cable","Gradient noise coupling into the cable","Static field exposure over the length of the scan","Cryogen contact from the vent line"],c:0,e:"Transmitted radiofrequency energy heats tissue, and it concentrates where a cable loops on itself, where insulation is damaged, or where a lead touches skin. Route cables so they cannot loop, keep them off skin, and retire damaged coils rather than taping them."},
{id:"u2l5-5",t:"u2l5",q:"Zipper artifacts have appeared across images from a magnetic resonance scanner. What should you check FIRST?",a:["The gradient amplifier output","The room's radiofrequency integrity: the door closed and latched, the finger stock, and every cable through the filter panel","The helium level and cold head","The reconstruction computer's software version"],c:1,e:"Those artifacts mean outside radiofrequency energy is reaching the receive chain, and the cheapest causes are a propped or unlatched door, failed door seals, and a cable run through a wall penetration instead of the filter panel. Work the room before the electronics."},

// u3l1
{id:"u3l1-1",t:"u3l1",q:"You replace a collimator assembly on a certified radiographic system. Whose obligation is it that the room still meets the federal performance standard?",a:["The manufacturer's only, because the system was certified when it was built","The state inspector's, at the next survey","Yours, because the standard binds anyone who modifies a certified system","Nobody's, until the system is sold again"],c:2,e:"21 CFR 1020 binds the manufacturer and anyone who modifies a certified system, so after a collimator, automatic exposure control, or high-voltage change the room must still meet every requirement when you leave, with the measurement in the record to prove it."},
{id:"u3l1-2",t:"u3l1",q:"Measuring tube housing leakage radiation during an acceptance test, what limit applies?",a:["Less than 500 mR/h at the tabletop","Less than 10 mR/h at 1 m from the housing","Less than 1 R/h at the collimator face","Less than 100 mR/h at 1 m from the housing"],c:3,e:"The federal standard holds housing leakage below 100 mR/h, about 1 mGy/h, measured at 1 m from the housing at the maximum continuous rating. It is leakage through the housing, not the useful beam, so it is measured with the shutters closed."},
{id:"u3l1-3",t:"u3l1",q:"During an acceptance test you lay lead over the selected automatic exposure control cell so the chamber never sees radiation. Where must the backup cut the exposure off?",a:["6 s or 600 mAs, whichever comes first","2 s or 200 mAs","10 s or 1000 mAs","When the operator releases the switch"],c:0,e:"The backup timer must end the exposure at 6 s or 600 mAs, whichever comes first, so a cell that never sees radiation cannot keep the beam on indefinitely. It is tested by putting lead over the selected cell."},
{id:"u3l1-4",t:"u3l1",q:"A replacement x-ray tube has been installed in an existing room. Which body must be notified so the machine stays properly registered?",a:["The accrediting organization","The state radiation control program","The manufacturer's regulatory affairs office","The hospital's information technology department"],c:1,e:"States register and inspect the machines in use, set posting and operator rules, and can prohibit use of a room. A new, replaced, or relocated tube is a registration event, and your paperwork feeds it; most state rules follow the shared suggested regulations written through CRCPD."},
{id:"u3l1-5",t:"u3l1",q:"A fluoroscope may have contributed to a patient injury, and the department wants the room back quickly. What is the correct FIRST action?",a:["Replace the suspect board and return the room to service","Run a full output test to see whether it is really at fault","Take the unit out of service and preserve it exactly as it is, logs and settings included","Clear the error log so the next fault is easier to see"],c:2,e:"A device that may have caused or contributed to a death or serious injury is evidence. Remove it from service physically, preserve settings, accessories, and logs, document and photograph, and hand it to risk management with its service history; repairing or testing it first destroys the evidence."},

// u3l2
{id:"u3l2-1",t:"u3l2",q:"Which statement correctly describes how mammography is regulated in the United States?",a:["It is accredited voluntarily, with no federal inspection","It follows the same requirements as general radiography","It is regulated only by state radiation control programs","A federal law makes the FDA the regulator, requiring certification, accreditation, annual inspection, an annual physicist survey, and a written quality control program"],c:3,e:"The Mammography Quality Standards Act puts mammography under the FDA, and it is the one modality whose quality control schedule is written into law. A facility needs a certificate, accreditation by an approved body such as the ACR, annual inspection, an annual medical physicist survey, qualified personnel, and a written program."},
{id:"u3l2-2",t:"u3l2",q:"On a mammography unit's quality control schedule, how often is a phantom image taken and scored?",a:["Weekly","Daily","Monthly","Quarterly"],c:0,e:"The phantom image is weekly, alongside daily detector or processor checks, a monthly visual checklist, quarterly repeat analysis or every 250 patients, semiannual compression force, and the annual physicist survey. Exam items ask which task sits at which interval."},
{id:"u3l2-3",t:"u3l2",q:"A weekly mammography phantom image scores three fibers, three speck groups, and three masses. How should that be read?",a:["A pass, since three of each is the minimum","A failure of the fiber minimum, because four fibers are required","A pass, because only the specks and masses are scored for accreditation","An inconclusive result that should be rescored by the physicist"],c:1,e:"The minimum to pass accreditation is four fibers, three speck groups, and three masses, and a score must not fall more than 0.5 from the previous image. A short fiber count is a stop-and-investigate event: look at detector calibration, target and filter selection, compression, and the beam path."},
{id:"u3l2-4",t:"u3l2",q:"After replacing a compression paddle drive, what must you verify before the mammography unit returns to clinical use?",a:["Only that the paddle moves smoothly through its travel","That compression reaches at least 50 lb to guarantee thin tissue","That power and manual modes both reach 25 to 40 lb, hold at least 15 seconds, and that power mode cannot exceed 40 lb","That the paddle releases automatically after 5 minutes"],c:2,e:"Compression force is verified with a scale in both modes against the 25 to 40 lb range with a hold of at least 15 seconds, and automatic mode must never exceed 40 lb. The measurement, the tolerance, and pass or fail all go in the record."},
{id:"u3l2-5",t:"u3l2",q:"You have replaced a major component in the imaging chain of a mammography unit. What else does compliance require before clinical use?",a:["Nothing beyond your own functional check","A state inspection","A new facility certificate","A medical physicist survey, documented, before the unit is used clinically"],c:3,e:"The physicist survey is annual and also required after a major component change, so deciding and documenting whether your replacement counts as major is part of the job. Getting that wrong leaves a working unit out of compliance."},

// u3l3
{id:"u3l3-1",t:"u3l3",q:"An accreditation surveyor arrives unannounced and asks about equipment management. What will they ask to see FIRST?",a:["The equipment inventory, then individual device records and the reasoning behind each interval","The manufacturer's service manuals","Staff schedules and overtime records","The department's capital budget"],c:0,e:"The Joint Commission's path is predictable: the inventory, then devices chosen from it, then each device's record, then why the interval is what it is. That is why completion rates, readings with their tolerances, and documented maintenance strategy are the things to keep current."},
{id:"u3l3-2",t:"u3l3",q:"A technician will use a high-level disinfectant on ultrasound probes. Which requirement covers the hazard information for that chemical?",a:["The equipment management standard","Hazard communication, which requires a container label and a safety data sheet available where the work is done","The conditions of participation","The radiation protection program"],c:1,e:"Hazard communication is an OSHA worker-protection requirement: a label on the container and an SDS available at the point of use, with training on both. In imaging the chemicals include disinfectants, cleaners, contrast agents, cryogens, and battery electrolyte."},
{id:"u3l3-3",t:"u3l3",q:"A standard governing hospital equipment management programs carries two organizations' names. What does each contribute?",a:["Both write the content, which is why both are named","One writes it and the other sells it","One accredits the development process and the other writes the content","One applies in the United States and the other internationally"],c:2,e:"ANSI accredits the process a standards developer follows while AAMI writes the content, so a standard with both names has an accredited process and developer-written technical requirements. Equipment management programs in hospitals are written against them."},
{id:"u3l3-4",t:"u3l3",q:"Which body publishes the recommended dose limits and the shielding reports a medical physicist designs barriers from?",a:["NEMA","CMS","OSHA","NCRP"],c:3,e:"NCRP publishes the dose limit recommendations adopted into practice and the shielding design reports barriers are computed from, which is why its limit table is the one worth memorizing. NEMA carries manufacturer standards such as focal spot tolerances."},
{id:"u3l3-5",t:"u3l3",q:"You discover that a shielded wall has been drilled through for a network cable and the penetration was never sealed. Who should hear about it, and why?",a:["The radiation safety officer, because the penetration changes the room's shielding design and the officer can require a survey and stop unsafe use","Nobody, if the cable fills the hole","The information technology department only, as it is their cable","The manufacturer of the imaging system"],c:0,e:"Shielding is designed from workload, use and occupancy factors, and distance, so an unsealed penetration is a change to that design. The radiation safety officer owns surveys, postings, and incident investigation and has the authority to stop unsafe practice, so this is reported before it becomes a finding."},

// u3l4
{id:"u3l4-1",t:"u3l4",q:"A manager asks what the computerized maintenance management system is supposed to contain. Which answer is complete?",a:["Only the preventive maintenance schedule","Inventory with risk class and intervals, work orders with findings and readings, PM schedules, parts and costs, service history, and attached documents","Work orders and parts costs, but not the inventory","A list of vendor contacts and contract numbers"],c:1,e:"The system is the database of record: every device by control number with its risk class and interval, every work order with symptom, finding, parts, labor, and readings, the due and overdue lists, costs, full service history, and documents such as physicist surveys and recall notices."},
{id:"u3l4-2",t:"u3l4",q:"Closing a work order after repairing an interlock, which entry is MOST useful to the next technician?",a:["No fault found, works now","Fixed rotor problem","Rotor-ready interlock contact open, contact block corroded; replaced contact block; interlock proved; room returned at 14:20","Replaced parts as needed"],c:2,e:"A usable history separates the finding from the action, names the part, records the verification, and gives the return-to-service time. Entries such as fixed or no fault found cannot be trended, cannot be defended in an incident review, and cannot feed a performance report."},
{id:"u3l4-3",t:"u3l4",q:"A manufacturer field notice names a model and a serial number range. What is the correct FIRST step?",a:["Ask the technologists which units they think are affected","Open one work order covering all affected devices together","Wait for the vendor to visit and identify the devices","Log the notice and search the inventory by model and serial range, including units in storage, loaners, and off-site clinics"],c:3,e:"The search is run against the inventory rather than from memory, because the devices that get missed are the ones not in daily service. Then one work order per device records the action and the date, and the notice closes only when every device is accounted for."},
{id:"u3l4-4",t:"u3l4",q:"Administration wants data to support replacing an aging fluoroscopy room. Which kind of report answers that, and what does it contain?",a:["A performance report, with mean time between failures, downtime by room, and cost of service against replacement value","A compliance report, with PM completion and overdue items","An inventory listing with control numbers","A copy of the physicist's annual survey"],c:0,e:"Compliance reports prove the program is managed and answer surveyors; performance reports show MTBF, downtime, and cost of service against replacement value, which is what a replacement request is argued from. Both depend on honestly closed work orders."},
{id:"u3l4-5",t:"u3l4",q:"An inspector asks for evidence that a safety-related repair was completed six months ago, and the work was done but never entered. How is that treated?",a:["As completed work, since the technician remembers doing it","As a finding, because undocumented work cannot be shown to have happened","As acceptable if the device is working now","As the inspector's problem to prove otherwise"],c:1,e:"The record is the only thing that speaks for the shop when nobody from the shop is in the room. A device with a gap in its history leaves that gap to be explained by somebody else's theory, which is why every call is opened and closed properly."},

// u4l1
{id:"u4l1-1",t:"u4l1",q:"Measuring a low-voltage rail in a generator cabinet, you find 24 V across an 8 ohm load resistor. What current is flowing?",a:["0.33 A","32 A","3 A","192 A"],c:2,e:"Ohm's law gives I = V / R = 24 / 8 = 3 A, and the power in that resistor is P = V x I = 24 x 3 = 72 W. Rearranging the same law three ways covers most circuit arithmetic on the exam."},
{id:"u4l1-2",t:"u4l1",q:"A generator is rated for a 1,000 mA station at 120 kVp. What electrical power does that station represent?",a:["12 kW","1.2 kW","1,200 kW","120 kW"],c:3,e:"Imaging power in kilowatts is (mA x kVp) / 1000, so (1000 x 120) / 1000 = 120 kW, multiplied by 0.7 on a single-phase generator. That figure is why an imaging room needs its own feeder and disconnect."},
{id:"u4l1-3",t:"u4l1",q:"An exposure switch produces nothing at all and no fault code appears on the console. Which circuit arrangement does that behavior point to?",a:["A series chain, because one open contact stops everything","A parallel branch, because one branch has opened","A short circuit across the supply","A failed display board"],c:0,e:"Interlocks sit in series with the exposure circuit, so a single open contact stops the exposure and frequently produces no code at all. A parallel fault leaves one item dead and the rest working, which is the opposite symptom."},
{id:"u4l1-4",t:"u4l1",q:"You need to walk an interlock chain on continuity. What condition must the equipment be in?",a:["Energized, so contacts are held in their working positions","Off, isolated, and with the capacitors discharged","Energized with the covers removed for access","In service mode with the interlocks bypassed"],c:1,e:"Resistance and continuity are measured only with the equipment off, isolated, and capacitors discharged, because a live circuit damages the meter and endangers you. Voltage is the measurement that requires a live circuit, taken across the component."},
{id:"u4l1-5",t:"u4l1",q:"Converting console values into base units before a calculation, which set is correct?",a:["70 kV is 7,000 V, 500 mA is 5 A, 10 ms is 0.1 s","70 kV is 700,000 V, 500 mA is 0.05 A, 10 ms is 0.001 s","70 kV is 70,000 V, 500 mA is 0.5 A, 10 ms is 0.01 s","70 kV is 70 V, 500 mA is 500 A, 10 ms is 10 s"],c:2,e:"Kilo multiplies by 1,000, milli divides by 1,000, and micro divides by a million, so 70 kV is 70,000 V, 500 mA is 0.5 A, and 10 ms is 0.01 s. Most arithmetic errors on these questions are prefix errors rather than algebra errors."},

// u4l2
{id:"u4l2-1",t:"u4l2",q:"Tracing a filter stage on a generator board, how do a capacitor and an inductor differ in what they pass?",a:["Both pass direct current freely","Both block alternating current","A capacitor blocks alternating current and passes direct current","A capacitor passes alternating current and blocks direct current, while an inductor opposes a change in current"],c:3,e:"A capacitor stores charge, so it passes alternating current and blocks direct current, and it smooths rectified voltage. An inductor opposes a change in current, which is what makes chokes, stator windings, and transformer windings behave as they do."},
{id:"u4l2-2",t:"u4l2",q:"A bleeder path in a cabinet is a 10 kohm resistor across a 100 microfarad capacitor. How long until the capacitor is essentially discharged?",a:["About 5 seconds","About 1 second","About 50 seconds","About 0.1 second"],c:0,e:"One time constant is tau = R x C = 10,000 ohm x 0.0001 F = 1 second, and the curve is essentially complete, about 99 percent, after five time constants, so about 5 seconds. The bleed-down interval in a service manual is this calculation."},
{id:"u4l2-3",t:"u4l2",q:"A rotor drive does nothing and the console shows no error. Measuring the SCR in that circuit, what two failure states should you expect to distinguish?",a:["Only an open circuit, since they cannot short","Shorted, passing power continuously, or open, passing nothing","Reversed polarity or correct polarity","High resistance or low capacitance"],c:1,e:"A silicon controlled rectifier conducts once its gate is triggered and stops when the current passes through zero, so it fails either shorted, passing power all the time, or open, passing none. Measure both sides of it before blaming the control board."},
{id:"u4l2-4",t:"u4l2",q:"Working from a service manual's marked test points, what does a reading that matches the printed value tell you?",a:["That the meter needs recalibration","That the board at that point is faulty","That everything upstream of that point is working, so the fault lies downstream","Nothing, because printed values are nominal"],c:2,e:"Each test point that reads its printed value proves the chain up to it, so each measurement halves the problem and the first mismatch names the stage. That is why reading the manual before opening a cabinet saves more time than any amount of probing."},
{id:"u4l2-5",t:"u4l2",q:"A generator is quoted as having about 1 percent voltage ripple. Which supply type does that describe, and what does it mean for exposures?",a:["Single-phase full-wave, so technique charts transfer directly","Three-phase six-pulse, with the least output of any type","Half-wave, which is the most efficient arrangement","High frequency, giving nearly constant potential and more output for the same settings"],c:3,e:"Ripple falls as pulses per cycle rise: 100 percent single-phase, about 14 percent six-pulse, about 4 percent twelve-pulse, and about 1 percent high frequency. Lower ripple means more quantity and a harder beam for the same settings, which is why a technique chart does not transfer between generator types."},

// u4l3
{id:"u4l3-1",t:"u4l3",q:"An autotransformer delivers 120 V to the primary of a step-up transformer whose turns ratio is 750 to 1. What appears across the tube?",a:["About 90 kVp","About 9 kVp","About 900 kVp","About 16 kVp"],c:0,e:"Secondary voltage equals primary voltage times the turns ratio: 120 x 750 = 90,000 V, which is 90 kVp. Current runs inversely, so the primary side carries high current while the secondary carries milliamperes."},
{id:"u4l3-2",t:"u4l3",q:"Which transformer in an imaging generator is a step-down type, and what does it supply?",a:["The one feeding the high-voltage rectifiers, supplying kilovolts","The filament transformer, supplying a few volts at about 4 A or more to heat the filament","The autotransformer, supplying the console logic","The isolation transformer, supplying the detector"],c:1,e:"A step-down transformer has fewer secondary turns, so it trades voltage for current: the filament supply delivers a few volts at about 4 A or more, and the heat it produces sets tube current. The step-up transformer makes the kilovolts instead."},
{id:"u4l3-3",t:"u4l3",q:"Why does a transformer do nothing when supplied with direct current?",a:["Because direct current cannot flow through copper windings","Because the core saturates instantly at any current","Because induction requires a changing magnetic field, and a steady current produces a steady field","Because rectifiers block the primary side"],c:2,e:"Induction depends on a change in the magnetic field passing through a conductor, so a steady field induces nothing. That is why the autotransformer and step-up transformer sit on the alternating side of a generator and the rectifiers come after them."},
{id:"u4l3-4",t:"u4l3",q:"Explaining to a new technician why an x-ray tube needs a dose program and a wireless access point does not, which statement is correct?",a:["X-rays travel faster than radio waves","X-rays have a longer wavelength and therefore more energy","Radio energy is blocked by building walls","X-ray photons carry enough energy to remove electrons from atoms, which radio photons cannot"],c:3,e:"Across the spectrum, shorter wavelength means higher frequency and more energy per photon, and only at the x-ray and gamma end does a single photon ionize. Intensity does not change that threshold, which is why ionizing radiation is regulated and radio energy is not."},
{id:"u4l3-5",t:"u4l3",q:"A tube's rotor runs up slowly and sounds rough, and the rotor-ready interlock is slow to satisfy. What does that MOST likely indicate?",a:["Bearing wear in the anode assembly, meaning the tube is near the end of its life","A failed collimator lamp circuit","A high line voltage condition","An incorrect focal spot selection"],c:0,e:"The anode is spun by an induction motor through the envelope, at about 3,400 rpm standard and about 10,000 rpm high speed, and heat travelling down the anode stem ruins its bearings. A noisy, slow run-up is the classic end-of-life sign, while no spin at all is usually the stator drive or its capacitor."},

// u4l4
{id:"u4l4-1",t:"u4l4",q:"Why is three-phase power used to feed the larger imaging generators?",a:["It is cheaper per kilowatt-hour than single-phase power","Its combined waveform never falls to zero, so it delivers heavy power with less current and much less voltage sag","It does not require a ground connection","It can be rectified without diodes"],c:1,e:"Three offset waveforms sum to a supply that never drops to zero, which moves large power with lower current and far less sag than single-phase. Facility distribution for imaging is commonly 480 VAC three-phase, stepped down to what each room needs."},
{id:"u4l4-2",t:"u4l4",q:"A relocated portable has been plugged into a corridor receptacle and now reads kilovolts low during exposures. What explains it?",a:["The detector gain calibration has expired","The tube has lost filtration","Voltage drop, because drop equals the exposure current times the line impedance of the supply path","The collimator is out of alignment"],c:2,e:"An exposure draws enormous momentary current, and the drop is that current times the impedance of everything between the transformer and the tube. A long, thin, or shared supply path sags exactly when the exposure needs voltage, which reads as low kilovolts or an aborted exposure."},
{id:"u4l4-3",t:"u4l4",q:"You find a room's line voltage compensator sitting at the end of its adjustment range. What does that tell you?",a:["The compensator has failed and should be replaced","The tube needs a warm-up series","The autotransformer taps are dirty","The building supply is out of tolerance, so the conversation belongs with facilities"],c:3,e:"The compensator corrects the incoming supply ahead of the autotransformer so one technique repeats all day. Sitting at the end of its travel means it has run out of correction, which indicts the building's power rather than the generator."},
{id:"u4l4-4",t:"u4l4",q:"Reading a room's electrical drawing, what distinguishes a delta connection from a wye connection?",a:["Delta is a closed triangle with no neutral, while wye joins three windings at a common point that becomes the neutral","Delta is used only for single-phase loads","Wye has no ground reference","Delta carries direct current and wye carries alternating current"],c:0,e:"At recognition level, delta is a closed triangle with no neutral and wye joins the windings at a common point that provides a neutral and two usable voltages. Which one a facility uses determines the available voltages and the grounding arrangement, and it is on the drawing rather than in your head."},
{id:"u4l4-5",t:"u4l4",q:"A technologist reports unexplained image noise in a room that also failed its leakage test. What single cause should you suspect?",a:["Two separate faults that happen to coincide","A grounding or bonding problem, because a lifted or high-resistance ground produces both leakage and noise","A detector pixel map that is out of date","Scatter from an uncollimated field"],c:1,e:"Grounding and bonding both keep fault and leakage current off people and keep the system electrically quiet, so one bad ground gives you an electrical safety failure and image noise together. Treat the pair of symptoms as one clue rather than two problems."},

// u4l5
{id:"u4l5-1",t:"u4l5",q:"Specifying power protection for a reading room and an archive server, which uninterruptible supply type gives the cleanest output?",a:["Standby, because it switches only when needed","Line-interactive, because it regulates and switches","Online double conversion, because the load always runs from the inverter with no switchover gap","A surge strip with a high joule rating"],c:2,e:"Online double conversion always runs the load through the inverter, so there is no transfer gap and the output is conditioned continuously. Line-interactive regulates and switches on loss, standby only switches, a line conditioner has no battery, and a surge strip is neither."},
{id:"u4l5-2",t:"u4l5",q:"During a building outage the console and computers stay up but no exposures can be made. Is that expected?",a:["No, the uninterruptible supply should carry the generator as well","Yes, because exposures are blocked by policy during outages","No, it indicates a failed transfer switch","Yes, the supply protects consoles, computers, and archives, not an exposure's momentary draw"],c:3,e:"A generator's momentary draw is far beyond what a room's uninterruptible supply is sized for, so what it buys is a study not lost, a database not corrupted mid-write, and a detector not left half-calibrated. Imaging stops; the computers survive."},
{id:"u4l5-3",t:"u4l5",q:"A system resets intermittently, usually under load, and three boards have already been replaced without success. What should you do now?",a:["Measure every low-voltage rail at its test point while the system is working and look for a sagging supply or a tired filter capacitor","Replace the remaining boards one at a time","Reload the system software","Request a replacement generator"],c:0,e:"A low or sagging rail imitates a dozen different faults, and boards are the most expensive wrong answer in the building. Measure each rail at the test point the manual names while the system is working, because a rail that has already collapsed tells you nothing about why."},
{id:"u4l5-4",t:"u4l5",q:"A detector is offline while the generator, console, and table all work normally. Where should you look?",a:["At the main contactor","At the detector's own supply rail, its cable, and the panel's power, since one distribution branch can fail alone","At the high-voltage cable terminations","At the room's disconnect"],c:1,e:"The incoming feed splits at the power distribution unit to generator, gantry, table, console, and computers, so a single branch or branch fuse can fail while everything else works. To the user it looks like total failure, which is why identifying live branches is the first split in a dead-system call."},
{id:"u4l5-5",t:"u4l5",q:"Before measuring inside a generator's high-voltage section, which sequence is correct?",a:["Bypass the interlock so the cabinet can stay energized for the measurement","Switch the console off, then measure immediately","Open and lock the disconnect, wait the manual's interval, discharge capacitors to ground with the proper tool, verify zero with a meter","Discharge the capacitors by making an exposure into a lead block"],c:2,e:"Capacitors and the high-voltage section hold energy after the disconnect is open, and the bleed-down interval in the manual is five time constants of the bleeder path. Lock, tag, wait, discharge to ground one capacitor at a time, verify zero, and never defeat an interlock to make a measurement convenient."},

// u4l6
{id:"u4l6-1",t:"u4l6",q:"Why can a portable x-ray unit make a full exposure from an ordinary ward receptacle?",a:["Because portable exposures use far less power than fixed rooms","Because portable units run on direct current from the building","Because the ward circuit is specially rated for imaging","Because it stores energy first in a battery pack or capacitor bank and releases it quickly, so the wall only refills the store"],c:3,e:"An exposure needs momentary power a general-purpose circuit cannot deliver, so the unit stores it and releases it in a fraction of a second. Many portables drive their wheels from the same pack, so a weak pack costs both exposures and transport."},
{id:"u4l6-2",t:"u4l6",q:"A sealed lead-acid pack has sat discharged in a storeroom for several months and now will not hold a charge. What happened?",a:["The pack sulfated while stored discharged and will not recover","The cells reversed polarity and need reconditioning","Memory effect reduced its usable capacity","The pack was overcharged in storage"],c:0,e:"A lead-acid pack left discharged sulfates and does not come back, which is why units live on the charger between cases. A deeply discharged lithium pack may likewise refuse to accept a charge, and neither chemistry may be substituted for the other."},
{id:"u4l6-3",t:"u4l6",q:"Exposures on a portable abort partway through when it is away from mains power, but the charge gauge shows full. What is the best test?",a:["Recharge it overnight and try again","Measure the pack voltage under load, because a tired pack reads nearly normal at rest and collapses under current","Replace the gauge","Check the collimator lamp circuit"],c:1,e:"Capacity fade shows only under load: at rest almost any failed pack looks fine. Measure under load, and trend exposures and run time per charge at every PM, because the trend is the warning and the indicator is not evidence."},
{id:"u4l6-4",t:"u4l6",q:"Staff report that one portable is always dead at the start of the morning shift. What should you check FIRST?",a:["The battery pack, which should be replaced","The tube's thermal history","Whether the unit is actually being plugged in, then the charger's output at the pack terminals and any charge interlock","The detector's calibration schedule"],c:2,e:"In order of likelihood it is staff not plugging it in, a charger not delivering current, a charge interlock holding the unit in a no-exposure state, a failed cord or inlet, and only then the pack. Check the receptacle too, since a hallway outlet on a switched circuit defeats any charging program."},
{id:"u4l6-5",t:"u4l6",q:"During a PM on an ultrasound cart you find the lithium-ion pack's case visibly swollen. What is the correct response?",a:["Return it to service and monitor it at the next visit","Discharge it fully and continue using it","Puncture the case to relieve the pressure","Remove the unit from service, isolate the pack, and follow the facility's disposal and transport rules"],c:3,e:"Swelling, heat, leakage, or a damaged case is a fire risk, so the pack comes out of service immediately and is isolated. Batteries are regulated waste and regulated cargo rather than trash, so storage, disposal, and shipping follow the facility's rules."}
);
